 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Directs the commissioner of health to establish at least 4 maternal-infant care centers in areas of need for infants suffering from drug withdrawal as a result of in utero exposure.",
 "title": "Directs the commissioner of health to establish at least 4 maternal-infant care centers in areas of need for infants suffering from drug withdrawal as a result of in utero exposure",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A5448",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A5448-2023",
     "printNo": "A5448",
     "session": 2023,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 123\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 5448-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the public health law, the social services law and\n      the family court act, in relation to establishing the maternal-in-\n      fant care centers pilot program; and providing for the  repeal  of\n      such provisions upon expiration thereof\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would require the Department of Health (DOH), in conjunction\nwith the Office of Addiction Services and Supports (OASAS), to establish\na pilot program of at least four designated or certified maternal-infant\ncare  centers  for  infants  under  the age of one experiencing neonatal\nabstinence syndrome, neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome, or other  drug\nwithdrawal  symptoms resulting from in utero exposure due to a substance\nuse disorder.\n \n  After recent CDC data revealed infant mortality rose  in  America  for\nthe  first  time  in two decades, it is more critical than ever to enact\npolicies that support babies and mothers. I am  proud  of  the  work  my\nadministration  has  done  to  address maternal and infant mortality and\ncommitted to making further progress in this space. However,  this  bill\nestablishes a pilot program that would require mothers and their infants\nto receive care in settings other than in their communities. New mothers\nand  their  babies  should  have  the  right to receive care while being\nsupported in their own communities.\n \n  To address this concern, I am directing  DOH  to  consult  with  other\nstate  agencies,  including  OASAS and the Office of Children and Family\nServices, to evaluate whether there are current  programs  and  services\nthat  can be further developed to provide this necessary care to mothers\nand infants without disrupting their living situations.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 123,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2023
   }
